adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop4+r7usb1+r7ios1+r7vol1+r7log1+r7ssh2+r7phosh2+r7apt1) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Redesign the console waybar into a compact 2-line status block
    (bottom-left, 9px monospace): line 1 is clock + battery + network/
    bluetooth, line 2 is cpu + memory. Dropped the launcher/taskbar/tray
    modules and fuzzel dependency - phoc has no window manager, so a
    second client from the launcher has nowhere sane to go, and taskbar
    has nothing meaningful to list with one client running.
    Two real data bugs found and fixed live, both via direct
    /sys-vs-waybar-output comparison, neither guessed:
    - Battery always read 0%: waybar's native battery module computes
      percentage from charge_now/charge_full, and this device's Samsung
      fuel-gauge driver reports charge_now as a broken near-zero stub
      (2 against a charge_full of 4300000) - always rounds to 0. The
      `capacity` sysfs attribute itself is correct. New
      /usr/libexec/r7-battery-status reads it directly; also treats
      ac/usb `online` as the charging indicator, not battery `status`
      alone, since a full battery reports "Full" (not "Charging") even
      with the charger still connected.
    - Network up/down always read 0 B/s: waybar's native
      {bandwidthUpBytes}/{bandwidthDownBytes} never updated even under a
      sustained 80MB+ download, confirmed live by diffing
      /sys/class/net/swlan0/statistics/rx_bytes directly against
      waybar's displayed value while downloading - the interface counter
      moved correctly (matching curl's own reported throughput almost
      exactly), waybar's computed value didn't, and a full trace log
      showed no netlink activity for it at all. New
      /usr/libexec/r7-network-status reads the sysfs counters directly
      and computes B/s from the delta since the last poll (state
      persisted in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR between invocations). Also found:
      the real default-route interface on this device is `swlan0`, not
      `wlan0` - confirmed via `ip route get`.
    Multi-line rendering itself needed a real workaround too: a single
    custom module's multi-line stdout (embedded \n) silently truncates
    to its first line on this waybar build (v0.12.0), confirmed with an
    isolated static-text test before assuming it was a script bug.
    Switched to waybar's native `group` module (orientation: vertical)
    wrapping per-line horizontal groups of real modules instead - stacks
    correctly, and was also the fix for an earlier failed attempt at
    three separate margin-offset bar surfaces from one process, which
    phoc could not position reliably (bars never lined up - not
    reproduced further since the group-based approach needs only one
    surface).

